[{"text":"which implies that \\(Z_A/Z_{\\xi}\\) must be finite so that one can fix \\(Z_\\xi = Z_A\\).","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"Decomposing the propagator into transverse and longitudinal parts and applying the Ward identity for the renormalized propagator as vertex function (1PI-part of the Green function)","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} q_\\mu \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = - \\frac{1}{\\xi} q^2 q^\\nu \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"we get","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} q_\\mu \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = q_\\mu \\Gamma_{R,L}^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = - \\frac{1}{\\xi} q^2 q^\\nu \\Rightarrow \\Gamma_{R,L} (q^2) = - \\frac{1}{\\xi} q^2 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"which means that the longitudinal part does not receive higher-order corrections.","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"From the Ward identity and the absence of poles in vertex functions one can follow that the transverse part of the vertex function vanishes at \\(q^2=0\\)","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_{R,T} (0) = \\Gamma_{R,L} (0) = 0. \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"The bare 2-point function for the massive vector field is defined as","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = -g^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2 - m^2) - \\frac{1-\\xi}{\\xi} q^{\\mu} q^{\\nu} - \\Pi^{\\mu \\nu} (q) \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"and the renormalized one reads","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = \\Gamma^{\\mu \\nu} (q) + \\;\\text{CT} \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"For convenience we also introduce","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\tilde{\\Gamma}^{\\mu \\nu}_R(q) = - \\Pi^{\\mu \\nu} (q) + \\;\\text{CT} \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"which corresponds to what one actually calculates when considering the sum of a bare amplitude and the corresponding counter-term.","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"The renormalization condition reads,","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\lim_{q^2 \\to m^2} \\left [ \\frac{\\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) \\varepsilon(q)_\\nu}{q^2 - m^2} = - \\varepsilon^{\\mu}(q) \\right ] \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"Multiplying both sides of the equality by q^2 we get","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2) \\varepsilon_\\nu (q) = - (q^2 - m^2) \\varepsilon^{\\mu}(q) \\overset{q^2 \\to m^2}{=} 0 \\Rightarrow \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2) \\varepsilon_\\nu(q) \\biggl|_{q^2=m^2} = 0. \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"Decomposition into transverse and longitudinal parts","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = \\left ( g^{\\mu \\nu} - \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2} \\right ) \\Gamma_{R,T} (q^2) + \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2} \\Gamma_{R,L} (q^2), \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"Contracting with the polarization vector we find","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2) \\varepsilon_\\nu (q) \\biggl |_{q^2=m^2} = 0 \\Rightarrow \\varepsilon^\\mu (q) \\, \\Gamma_{R,T} (m^2) = 0 \\Rightarrow \\Gamma_{R,T} (m^2) = 0 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"Plugging the decomposition into the renormalization condition and choosing the vector to be transverse we end up with","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\lim_{q^2 \\to m^2} \\left [ \\frac{\\varepsilon_{\\mu}(q) \\Gamma_{R,T} (q)}{q^2 -m ^2} = - \\varepsilon_{\\mu}(q) \\right ] \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"Using","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_{R,T} (q^2) = \\Gamma_{R,T} (m^2) + (q^2 - m^2) \\Gamma'_{R,T} (m^2) + \\mathcal{O}( (q^2 - m^2)^2) \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"we arrive at","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_{R,T}(m^2) = 0, \\quad \\Gamma_{R,T}'(m^2) = -1 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"which is equivalent to","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\tilde{\\Gamma}_{R,T}(m^2) = 0, \\quad \\tilde{\\Gamma}_{R,T}'(m^2) = 0 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massive-vector-field-renormalization","section":"Massive vector field renormalization"},{"text":"The bare 2-point vertex function for the fermion field is defined as","anchor":"fermion-field-renormalization","section":"Fermion field renormalization"}]